          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Albert Camus</strong><br>\nAlbert Camus was born on November 7, 1913, in Mondovi, Algeria. His father died in World War I, and he was raised in poverty by his hearing-impaired mother. Despite these challenges, Camus excelled in school and received a scholarship to the University of Algiers. However, his education was interrupted by tuberculosis, which plagued his health for years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Works</strong><br>\nCamus became known for his contributions to philosophy, particularly Absurdism, which suggests that human life is meaningless without religious belief. He authored essays, novels, and plays that expressed his philosophical ideas. His most famous works include \"The Stranger,\" \"The Plague,\" and \"The Myth of Sisyphus.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Stranger</strong><br>\nThe Stranger is one of Camus's most celebrated novels. It tells the story of Meursault, an indifferent French Algerian who is emotionally detached from society. The book explores themes of existentialism and absurdity, questioning the nature of morality and the meaning of life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
