          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Plague</strong><br>\nIn \"The Plague,\" Camus used the setting of a deadly epidemic in the Algerian city of Oran to explore isolation, human endurance, and the struggle against an irrational universe. The novel is seen as a metaphor for the French Resistance against Nazi occupation, which Camus was involved with during World War II.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Myth of Sisyphus</strong><br>\nThe Myth of Sisyphus is an influential philosophical essay by Camus, in which he introduces his philosophy of the absurd. He uses the Greek myth of Sisyphus, who was condemned to roll a boulder up a hill only for it to roll down each time, as a symbol of human persistence in the face of the absurd.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nAlbert Camus was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957 at the age of 44. The Swedish Academy recognized his work which \"illuminates the problems of the human conscience in our times.\" He was the second-youngest recipient of the prize at the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
