            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Views and Activism</strong><br>\nCamus was a vocal critic of totalitarianism and passionately defended human rights. He spoke out against the Soviet Union's policies and supported individual freedom. His political views often placed him at odds with other intellectuals of his time, like Jean-Paul Sartre.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nCamus died in a car accident on January 4, 1960, at the age of 46. His death was a significant loss to the literary world. However, his ideas and works continue to influence philosophy, literature, and discussions on the human condition.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Camus Today</strong><br>\nToday, Albert Camus is celebrated as a significant figure in literature and philosophy. His works remain relevant as they address universal questions of existence, freedom, and ethics. His writing is appreciated for its clear, forceful prose and continues to be studied and enjoyed worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
